LICENCE NOT YET SET — PLACEHOLDER
=================================

*** This repository must not be made public until this file is replaced. ***

Nature Communications requires that custom code supporting a publication be
released under a recognised open-source licence, and the Code Availability
statement for this manuscript points readers to
https://github.com/RSCJacksonLab. A repository with no licence is, by default,
"all rights reserved" — readers cannot legally reuse it, which does not satisfy
that requirement.

Decision needed from the Jackson lab / ANU:

  Option A (recommended, and the usual choice for a methods repository)
      MIT for the code in code/, and CC-BY-4.0 for the derived tables in
      data/ and results/. Permissive, citation-preserving, and compatible
      with the Zenodo deposit (10.5281/zenodo.21828480), which should carry
      the same terms.

  Option B
      MIT for everything. Simplest; one file covers code and data.

  Option C
      GPL-3.0. Only if the lab or ANU requires derivative works to remain
      open. Note this is more restrictive than most reviewers expect for
      analysis code and can deter reuse.

Points to confirm before choosing:

  - ANU / ARC Centre of Excellence policy on IP in published research code.
  - That the licence here matches the licence attached to the Zenodo record;
    a mismatch between the two is a common and avoidable reviewer query.
  - Third-party material redistributed here carries its own terms and is not
    covered by whichever licence you choose: CAZy-derived tables
    (data/cazy/), UniProt and InterPro annotations, and SignalP 6.0 output
    (SignalP is free for academic use only). Note these in the licence file
    or in docs/data_sources.md.

Once decided, replace this file with the full licence text (github.com/
licenses or choosealicense.com), set the `license:` field in CITATION.cff,
and state the licence in the README.
